= Explanation — Simple =

An '''explanation''' tells us why or how something happens.

A description tells us what happened.

An explanation tries to show the reason, cause, mechanism, or pattern behind it.

For example:

<blockquote>The metal expanded.</blockquote>

is a description.

<blockquote>The metal expanded because heating increased the motion and spacing of its particles.</blockquote>

is an explanation.

Different explanations can answer different questions.

A causal explanation may tell us what produced an event.

A mechanistic explanation may show how parts interact.

A mathematical explanation may show how a result follows from a structure or equation.

A [[Scientific model/Simple|scientific model]] can also help explain a system.

An explanation can sound convincing and still be wrong. It needs [[Evidence/Simple|evidence]] and must fit what we know about the system.

